# RelayDisplay — repository ignore rules
#
# Grouped by why something is excluded rather than by file extension, because the reason is what
# tells you whether a new rule belongs here. Three groups matter most:
#
#   * generated output — reproducible from source, so committing it only creates merge conflicts
#   * machine-local state — absolute paths and SDK locations that are wrong on every other machine
#   * secrets — signing material and captured device data, which must never reach a remote
#
# Anything needed to *build* the project from a clean clone stays tracked. In particular the
# Gradle wrapper jar is committed on purpose; see the note at the bottom.


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gradle and build output
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

.gradle/
build/
!src/**/build/
captures/
.cxx/
.externalNativeBuild/

# Kotlin 2.x writes per-build session files here. Purely local, and new since the Android Studio
# template that this project's first ignore file came from.
.kotlin/

# Gradle's own local overrides. gradle.properties IS tracked (it carries real build settings);
# only the per-developer override file is not.
gradle-app.setting
!gradle-wrapper.properties


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Machine-local configuration
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Points at this machine's Android SDK. Android Studio regenerates it on first open.
local.properties

# Android Studio project state. Formatting rules moved to .editorconfig, which every editor and
# ktlint honour, so nothing under .idea needs tracking any more.
.idea/
*.iml
*.bak
*.tmp
hs_err_pid*.log

# Other editors
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing material and secrets — never commit any of this
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The app's own device identity lives in the Android Keystore on each phone and never touches the
# filesystem, so nothing here is about that. These rules are about *release signing*: a leaked
# keystore lets anyone ship an update that Android will install over a real one.

*.jks
*.keystore
keystore.properties
signing.properties
key.properties
*.p12
*.pfx
*.pem
*.der
release/

# Signing material decoded at build time. The release workflow writes the keystore under
# $RUNNER_TEMP, outside the workspace, so it can never be swept into an artefact upload -- these
# patterns are a second line of defence for anyone decoding one locally.
release-signing.jks
*-signing.jks
signing-key.*
*.keystore.b64
*.jks.b64

# Cloud/backend config files. This app has no server side and adding one would be a design
# change, but these are listed so an accidental drop-in cannot be committed silently.
google-services.json
firebase-*.json
*.plist


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build artefacts that should be attached to a release, not committed
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.apk
*.aab
*.ap_
*.dex
*.class
output-metadata.json

# R8/ProGuard output. mapping.txt is needed to read a release crash, but it belongs with the
# release artefact, not in git.
mapping.txt
seeds.txt
usage.txt
proguard/

# Staged release artefacts. Built by the workflow, attached to the GitHub Release, never tracked.
/dist/
SHA256SUMS

# Lint and test reports are regenerated by the tasks that produce them.
lint-results*.html
lint-results*.xml
lint-results*.sarif
lint-results*.txt


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Captured device data
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# diagnostics/ has its own rules covering logs/. These catch the same material anywhere else:
# device captures contain local IP addresses, device names and peer fingerprints.

*.hprof
*.trace
*.perfetto-trace
heapdump*
/screenshots/
/diagnostics/logs/


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operating system clutter
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
Thumbs.db:encryptable
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kept on purpose
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar is how ./gradlew bootstraps a clean clone. Many generic
# Android ignore templates exclude *.jar and silently break the wrapper; this re-includes it so
# that cannot happen here.

!gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar
